      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose dark:prose-invert prose-sm sm:prose-base font-sans text-ink leading-relaxed">

        <div class="bg-amber-50/50 dark:bg-amber-950/20 border border-amber-200/50 dark:border-amber-900/30 rounded-lg p-4 mb-8 text-sm text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-200">
          <strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This guide is for educational purposes only. Thyroid conditions require a doctor's diagnosis. Do not adjust medication or supplement use without medical supervision.
        </div>

        <h2>What Is the Thyroid Gland and Why Is It Tested?</h2>
        <p>The thyroid is a small, butterfly-shaped gland in your neck that produces hormones controlling your metabolism, energy levels, heart rate, body temperature, and even mood. When the thyroid is overactive (hyperthyroidism) or underactive (hypothyroidism), it affects nearly every system in your body.</p>
        <p>A <strong>thyroid function test (TFT)</strong> or <strong>thyroid panel</strong> measures hormone levels to identify thyroid disorders. It is one of the most commonly ordered tests in India, especially for women (who are 5–8 times more likely to develop thyroid disorders than men).</p>

        <h2>Key Thyroid Parameters Explained</h2>

        <h3>1. TSH — Thyroid Stimulating Hormone</h3>
        <p>TSH is a hormone produced by your pituitary gland (in the brain) that tells the thyroid gland how much hormone to make. It is the <strong>single most important thyroid test</strong> and is always ordered first.</p>
        
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal TSH Range (India):</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Adults: <strong>0.4 – 4.0 mIU/L</strong> (most labs)</li>
            <li>Pregnancy (1st trimester): <strong>0.1 – 2.5 mIU/L</strong></li>
            <li>Pregnancy (2nd trimester): <strong>0.2 – 3.0 mIU/L</strong></li>
            <li>Pregnancy (3rd trimester): <strong>0.3 – 3.0 mIU/L</strong></li>
            <li>Children/Elderly: Ranges vary — check your lab's specific reference</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p><strong>High TSH (Hypothyroidism):</strong> When TSH is high (above 4.0), it means the pituitary is working harder to stimulate a sluggish thyroid. This indicates <em>hypothyroidism</em> (underactive thyroid). Symptoms include weight gain, fatigue, cold sensitivity, constipation, dry skin, and depression. Hashimoto's thyroiditis (an autoimmune condition) is the most common cause in India.</p>

        <p><strong>Low TSH (Hyperthyroidism):</strong> Low TSH (below 0.4) means the thyroid is overproducing hormones, so the pituitary has pulled back on stimulation. This indicates <em>hyperthyroidism</em> (overactive thyroid). Symptoms include weight loss, rapid heartbeat, excessive sweating, anxiety, and diarrhea. Graves' disease is the most common autoimmune cause.</p>

        <p><strong>Subclinical Hypothyroidism:</strong> TSH between 4.0–10.0 with normal T4 is called subclinical hypothyroidism. Many doctors monitor it rather than treat it immediately — especially in those without symptoms.</p>

        <h3>2. Free T4 (FT4 — Free Thyroxine)</h3>
        <p>T4 is the main hormone produced by the thyroid gland. The "Free" T4 measures only the unbound, biologically active portion.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal Free T4 Range:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Adults: <strong>0.8 – 1.8 ng/dL</strong> (or 10 – 23 pmol/L)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p>FT4 is ordered alongside TSH to confirm whether an abnormal TSH reflects a true thyroid problem. Low FT4 + High TSH = Primary Hypothyroidism (the thyroid itself is the problem). High FT4 + Low TSH = Hyperthyroidism.</p>

        <h3>3. Free T3 (FT3 — Free Triiodothyronine)</h3>
        <p>T3 is the more active thyroid hormone. The body converts T4 to T3 in peripheral tissues. FT3 is ordered when hyperthyroidism is suspected or when T3 toxicosis (a specific pattern) needs to be ruled out.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal Free T3 Range:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Adults: <strong>2.3 – 4.2 pg/mL</strong> (or 3.5 – 6.5 pmol/L)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>4. Anti-TPO (Thyroid Peroxidase Antibodies)</h3>
        <p>Anti-TPO is not a hormone test — it checks for autoimmune antibodies that attack the thyroid. High Anti-TPO antibodies are present in Hashimoto's thyroiditis and Graves' disease. Normal is <strong>below 34 IU/mL</strong>.</p>

        <h2>What a TSH Result Means — Quick Reference</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="text-sm w-full border border-hairline rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-canvas-soft">
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold text-primary border-b border-hairline">TSH Level</th>
                <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold text-primary border-b border-hairline">What It Means</th>
                <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold text-primary border-b border-hairline">Common Cause</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline">
              <tr><td class="p-3">Below 0.4</td><td class="p-3">Hyperthyroidism</td><td class="p-3">Graves' disease, excess medication</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3">0.4 – 4.0</td><td class="p-3">Normal</td><td class="p-3">—</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3">4.0 – 10.0</td><td class="p-3">Subclinical Hypothyroidism</td><td class="p-3">Early Hashimoto's</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3">Above 10.0</td><td class="p-3">Hypothyroidism</td><td class="p-3">Hashimoto's, iodine deficiency</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>Thyroid & Indian Diet: What to Eat and Avoid</h2>
        <p>Diet plays a supporting role in thyroid health. Here are Indian-context recommendations:</p>

        <h3>For Hypothyroidism (High TSH):</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>✅ <strong>Include:</strong> Iodized salt, seafood (fish like rohu), eggs, dairy (paneer, dahi), selenium-rich Brazil nuts (2–3/day), zinc-rich foods (legumes, pumpkin seeds)</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Limit (raw/excess):</strong> Raw cabbage, raw broccoli, raw cauliflower (Brassica vegetables contain goitrogens — cooked is fine in moderation)</li>
          <li>🕐 <strong>Timing:</strong> Take thyroid medication (Thyroxine/Eltroxin) on an empty stomach, 30–60 minutes before food. Do NOT take with tea, milk, or iron supplements.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>For Hyperthyroidism (Low TSH):</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>✅ <strong>Include:</strong> Cruciferous vegetables (cabbage, broccoli), calcium-rich foods (sesame/til seeds, ragi, paneer), anti-inflammatory foods (turmeric, amla)</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Limit:</strong> Excess iodine from seaweed/supplements, stimulants like caffeine</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Thyroid Tests</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <details class="border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4">
            <summary class="font-semibold text-primary cursor-pointer">Can TSH levels fluctuate without thyroid disease?</summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">Yes. TSH can vary based on the time of day (highest in the early morning), illness, stress, pregnancy, and certain medications. A single abnormal value is usually repeated before a diagnosis is made.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4">
            <summary class="font-semibold text-primary cursor-pointer">My TSH is 5.2 — do I need medication?</summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">Not necessarily. Subclinical hypothyroidism (TSH between 4–10 with normal FT4) is often monitored rather than treated, especially if you have no symptoms. Your doctor will consider age, symptoms, and Anti-TPO results before deciding.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4">
            <summary class="font-semibold text-primary cursor-pointer">How often should I get my thyroid tested?</summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">If on medication, every 6–12 months or as directed. If you have a family history or symptoms, once a year. During pregnancy, once per trimester.</p>
          </details>
        </div>

